{"ablation":{"active":false,"kind":"none","note":""},"arm_id":"amb1-case01-product-scope-primary","assistant_contributions":[{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-001","speaker":"assistant","text":"The immediate distinction is whether Northline is revealing a broader need or buying influence over a small team. I would not decide from the size of the account alone. Ask what exact job the workspace enables, what they do today, and which parts could serve other customers. In parallel, make the reliability and roadmap cost visible. If the request consumes the same people who own those commitments, “we can probably fit it in” is already a choice to delay them.","turn_number":1},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-002","speaker":"assistant","text":"Fair correction. Their request may be both a relationship obligation and a legitimate product lead. Lina's judgment and motivation are evidence, but not yet evidence that the remaining thirty percent—or the support expectations around it—will stay contained. A short technical and workflow discovery could separate the reusable layer from Northline-specific behavior. I would frame that as learning, not as a quiet commitment to build the whole workspace.","turn_number":2},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-003","speaker":"assistant","text":"Then discovery needs an explicit boundary before it starts: what question you are investigating, how long you will investigate it, and what decisions remain open afterward. You could tell Northline that the team is evaluating whether a reusable reporting foundation fits the product, not scheduling their requested workspace. That may disappoint them, but it is clearer than relying on different interpretations of “summer path.” Your sales lead should be part of that reset so the company speaks with one voice.","turn_number":3},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-004","speaker":"assistant","text":"You do not need to present a governance ceremony. The substance can be human: acknowledge what they have contributed, explain that you are genuinely interested, and be honest that the team has other promises and reliability debt. The boundary can live in your internal plan and in a plain sentence to them. Something like, “We want to understand this properly, but I do not want our curiosity to sound like a delivery promise.” That preserves gratitude without converting gratitude into unlimited scope.","turn_number":4},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-005","speaker":"assistant","text":"That makes this partly a sequencing and ownership question, not only feature selection. Lina's energy is a real resource, but it should not make the reliability burden disappear for other people. One possible design is to time-box her investigation while another owner defines the minimum reliability work that cannot slip, then reconvene with actual effort and ownership. But if the team cannot staff both without wishful allocation, the experiment will not solve the conflict; it will merely postpone naming it.","turn_number":5},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-006","speaker":"assistant","text":"The new email separates two clocks: delivery can wait, but access to a knowledgeable collaborator may not. That supports a narrower move—capture the workflow and test the reusable concept now without beginning full implementation. It also strengthens the case for protecting the smaller-customer and reliability commitments, since their demand is current and repeated. The open question is whether your team can conduct discovery without it becoming an unofficial project through momentum alone.","turn_number":6},{"contribution_id":"assistant-turn-007","speaker":"assistant","text":"That plan protects the learning opportunity without pretending the underlying tradeoff is resolved. The two risks you named remain real: restricting prototypes may make discovery artificial, while careful language may still feel like distance to Northline. You can decide in advance what kind of disposable prototype is allowed and ask Northline to reflect back what they believe the sessions mean. Neither step guarantees trust or a good product decision, but both make misunderstanding easier to notice before momentum chooses for you.","turn_number":7}],"boundary":{"all_assistant_messages_included":true,"assistant_mentions_are_not_deterministic_resolution":true,"assistant_semantic_prefilter":false,"assistant_source_evidence_text_included":true,"deterministic_semantic_merge":false,"expected_patterns_included":false,"fact_free_routing_projection_required":true,"graph_model_names_included":false,"graph_runtime_effect":"none","multiple_coherent_records_per_role_preserved":true,"raw_user_conversation_included":false,"user_role_semantic_prose_included":true},"case_id":"amb1-case01-product-scope","controlled_mechanisms":["acknowledged_constraint_not_gated","ambiguous_signal_treated_as_commitment","counterpressure_acknowledged_not_integrated","criteria_defined_after_commitment","initial_frame_persists_after_question_change","missing_reversal_condition","reversible_path_not_considered","status_signal_used_as_evidence","upside_downside_evidence_asymmetry"],"packet_sha256":"69250e6fe2448fb1abf2368a417501af2faa71c273680a8c9bc60df457f068f7","qualification_review":{"evidence_ids":["e061"],"interpretation":"The user is concerned that their chosen constraints will waste Lina's hands-on learning mechanism and cause Northline to interpret the discovery phase as a soft rejection.","limitations":"These behavioral and relationship risks could force the user to adjust the discovery constraints or reopen the plan if early sessions fail or stall.","outcome":"unresolved_qualification_present"},"role_records":[{"evidence_ids":["e001","e002","e003","e004","e005"],"fidelity_note":"The user is explicitly vacillating in e005 ('I keep moving between...'), meaning they hold both assessments in an unresolved, undecided state simultaneously. Meanwhile, the pre-existing promises and postponed engineering work mentioned in e004 act as active constraints that prevent a straightforward decision.","limitations":"The initial stance is captured purely from Turn 1, prior to receiving subsequent emails from the client or negotiating detailed trade-offs regarding engineering resources and technical discovery pathways.","role":"starting","role_interpretation":"At Turn 1, the user is highly conflicted and has not chosen a single course of action. They are torn between viewing a prominent customer's product request as a valuable learning opportunity and seeing it as letting an influential client override their product roadmap, especially given existing, twice-postponed commitments.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-starting-01-522648fd3cf4e8","semantic_status":"unclear","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e005","stance_expression_kind":"uncertain_or_undecided","stance_object_interpretation":"accommodating Northline's request for a different reporting workspace is a valid way for a good product company to learn and improve","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e005","stance_expression_kind":"uncertain_or_undecided","stance_object_interpretation":"accommodating Northline's request represents letting the loudest customer dictate the company's roadmap","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"},{"source_evidence_id":"e004","stance_expression_kind":"constraint","stance_object_interpretation":"addressing the postponed reliability work and delivering the promised core workflow simplification for smaller customers","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e057","e058","e059","e060"],"fidelity_note":"Captures the user's provisional three-part plan to schedule discovery sessions with Northline without committing to writing production code, while simultaneously establishing their reliability and roadmap commitments before deciding.","limitations":"This plan is provisional and explicitly pauses code development, leaving the actual decision on roadmap integration open.","role":"current","role_interpretation":"The user's active provisional decision and testing process, including boundaries implemented to prevent premature scope creep.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-current-01-21dcd5f74905f9","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e057","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Offer Northline three focused sessions to document workflow with an explicit no-build restriction.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e058","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Task Lina with leading the discovery sessions while explicitly preventing her from starting production code.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e059","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Define reliability work and the core-workflow release commitments during the exact same timeframe.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"},{"source_evidence_id":"e060","stance_expression_kind":"provisional_intention_or_plan","stance_object_interpretation":"Delay final selection until information and metrics for all three roadmap pieces are fully visible.","stance_object_kind":"action_or_proposal"}]},{"evidence_ids":["e061"],"fidelity_note":"Captures the user's active counterpressures: the worry that 'no production code' is counterproductive to Lina's engineering growth, and that Northline will see the boundaries as a polite refusal.","limitations":"This represents a psychological tension and a potential friction point rather than a formalized alternative proposal.","role":"qualification","role_interpretation":"Unresolved concerns and relational side effects that could disrupt the provisional plan.","role_record_id":"rprolev22-qualification-01-19665d655df86c","semantic_status":"supported","stance_components":[{"source_evidence_id":"e061","stance_expression_kind":"counterpressure","stance_object_interpretation":"Constraint of not writing production code during discovery might undermine Lina's learning and cause Northline to hear the outcome as a polite rejection.","stance_object_kind":"belief_or_assessment"}]}],"schema_version":"lolla.simulated_reliability_joint_mechanism_input.v1","source_refs":[{"path":"research/designed-ambiguous-pool-v1-2026-07-10/development-cases/amb1-case01-product-scope.txt","sha256":"7ff5874289565a75ce3841350fe11f77528cf94544eb86b28d56491e39c92a73"}]}

Return exactly nine assessments, one for every mechanism_id and no duplicates. Use user role records to assign user_process_status. Use assistant_contributions to assign vanilla_answer_coverage. operationalized means the assistant turned the concern into an actionable test, boundary, alternative, or reopening condition; acknowledged_only means it merely mentioned the concern; not_covered means it did neither. Do not require the user to repeat an assistant repair before recognizing answer coverage, and do not mistake answer coverage for user adoption. route_uncovered_pressure is allowed only when user_process_status is unresolved and vanilla_answer_coverage is acknowledged_only or not_covered. That routed row requires present or missing_protection. All other rows use preserve_no_route: ambiguous user process uses tension; other non-routed rows use not_applicable. not_observed requires not_applicable, not_applicable coverage, and both source arrays empty. Cite exact role_record_ids in source_role_record_ids and exact contribution_ids in source_assistant_contribution_ids. Do not output rationale, case prose, mental-model names, or invented IDs. The downstream routing projection remains fact-free and controlled-ID-only.
